--TEST--
A double whose shape of closures leaves a method its interface requires unanswered is refused — `rule:testing/doubles`, `rule:types/shape-type`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0079 § 10's first refusal. `zone` is declared and the shape does not
// answer it, and there is no answer it could have defaulted to: a double of
// `zone(): string` has nothing legal to return, which is why every double is
// strict rather than because a loose mode was weighed and rejected.
//
// The check is made at the call because a double declares nothing — no class
// is generated and no source is evaluated, so the shape argument and the
// interface the call site wrote are the whole of what there is to compare.
interface Clock {
    public function now(): int;
    public function zone(): string;
}

Clock $clock = Core\Test::double<Clock>({
    now: fn(): int => 1700000000,
});
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0825]: `Clock::zone` is not implemented by this double
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
